#N canvas 20 20 2900 1560 12;
#X declare -lib pdlua;
#X text 20 20 m_nano -- the nanoKONTROL's device layer. Decodes CC into named controls on param and knows nothing about what they control. ref/device/nanokontrol.md, f 58;
#X text 20 130 creation arg 1: the Pd channel the nano's own channel 1 lands on (17 on the device) \, required. The transport row is that channel plus one \, and this accepts both. inlets: none. outlets: none., f 58;
#X obj 20 400 ctlin;
#X obj 660 470 - \$1;
#X obj 660 520 t f f;
#X obj 660 570 >= 0;
#X obj 820 570 < 2;
#X obj 660 620 *;
#X obj 660 670 s \$0-nano-on;
#X text 660 720 Two channels \, one flag: the offset is 0 for the control groups and 1 for the transport row \, so the gate is 0 <= offset < 2 and the Launchpad (16 below) and Pd's port 3 (16 above) fall outside it., f 44;
#X obj 1180 400 loadbang;
#X obj 1180 440 del 2000;
#X obj 1180 480 f \$1;
#X obj 1180 520 print m_nano-control-channel;
#X text 1180 570 C-9, f 40;
#X text 20 940 Nothing here drives mode \, start or stop. The mode is the Launchpad's left column \, and the map gives xport-2 start and xport-5 stop., f 58;
#X text 860 900 ctlin fires right to left: channel \, controller \, then the value that triggers (item 23)., f 46;
#X obj 2100 300 c_devid 66;
#X obj 2380 300 r \$0-nano-on;
#X obj 2380 360 select 1;
#X obj 1700 480 c_presence m_nano 3 60;
#X msg 1700 560 240 \, 126 \, 127 \, 6 \, 1 \, 247;
#X obj 1700 780 midiout;
#X obj 2100 480 loadbang;
#X obj 2100 530 f \$1;
#X obj 2100 580 - 1;
#X obj 2100 630 div 16;
#X obj 2100 680 + 1;
#X text 1700 860 c_devid 66 is KORG's manufacturer byte \, 0x42 (item 249). The 60 ms stagger keeps the two inquiry replies \, the Launchpad's and this one \, from interleaving on the one sysexin (ref/module/presence.md)., f 64;
#X text 1700 1120 The port is derived from the channel block \, (block-1)/16+1 \, rather than passed in: a second argument would be a second source of truth. m_launchpad uses its block as its port \, which works only because both are 1 \, so do not copy it., f 64;
#X text 2500 860 The second source of liveness: any CC in this file's own block \, so a nano being played stays present. The select's reject goes nowhere on purpose: ctlin is omni \, and a 0 is evidence about another device., f 44;
#X text 2500 1300 c_presence's second outlet stays unconnected: this file owns no surface and no per-event state \, so a loss has nothing to drop. c_presence reports it and u_present re-wires., f 44;
#X obj 20 500 spigot;
#X obj 160 460 r \$0-nano-on;
#X obj 20 560 list prepend;
#X obj 20 620 m_nano-lua;
#X obj 20 680 s param;
#X obj 160 680 s err;
#X msg 300 680 0;
#X obj 400 420 loadbang;
#X obj 400 460 t b b;
#X msg 520 540 init;
#X obj 400 700 spigot 1;
#X msg 400 750 fail m_nano no-lua;
#X obj 400 800 s err;
#X obj 2100 200 declare -lib pdlua;
#X text 20 1100 The decode is Lua: m_nano-lua names a CC by its tens -- slider \, knob \, btn-t \, btn-b \, xport -- and its units \, passes a slider's or knob's value \, sends a button's press as 1 and drops its release \, and warns cc-N-unmapped for any other CC. The rules are m_nano.lua \, tested without Pd by test/gate/lua/m_nano_test.lua (ref/device/nanokontrol.md). param only: the row a control draws is u_map's (item 317)., f 60;
#X text 20 1400 The spigot is the channel gate \, so only the nano's two channels reach the Lua. list prepend holds the controller cold \, so the value \, which fires last \, arrives as <controller> <value>., f 60;
#X text 1180 1000 At load the Lua answers init with a bang on its last outlet \, which shuts the spigot. No bang means it never loaded -- pdlua missing \, or an error in the Lua -- and fail m_nano no-lua says so on the screen: every control on the nano is dead. A Lua error is fail m_nano lua-<rule> (C-12)., f 60;
